====================================================================================================
STEP 6 -- strongest surviving statement and first transfer implication
====================================================================================================
  THEOREM (conditional, this run):
    OR composition mu = 1 - prod_i (1 - p_i), p_i'(0) = b_i, p_i(0) = 0,
    p_i(oo) = 1, b_i > 0  =>  mu'(0) = sum_i b_i (exact), and the deep
    spherical matching gives kappa = a0/s = 1/(sum_i b_i) with
    a0 = s/(sum_i b_i) on whichever footing fixes s.
  COUNTEREXAMPLE (this run):  (b1, b2) = (1, 2) gives mu'(0) = 3, kappa
    = 1/3 != 1/2 on both footings -- the channel count 2 alone does NOT
    force kappa = 1/2; the composition does NOT remove the per-channel
    slope freedom.
  EQUAL-SLOPE PREMISE NEEDED FOR kappa = 1/n:  sum_i b_i = n; the only
    premise in the framework's action class that supplies it is the
    unit-slope fraction identity p_i'(0) = 1 (PD08 step 3, k01 no-go:
    one scale s, no second coefficient), which EXCLUDES unequal slopes.
  FIRST TRANSFER IMPLICATION: the delta-function counting argument
    (PD01 A1: slope = count for equal channels) needs the map from the
    metric's two static channels to per-channel slopes b_i = 1; if a
    future derivation yields b1 + b2 = 2 without unit slopes (e.g.
    b1 = 1/2, b2 = 3/2), the count reading of PD01 is replaced by a
    slope-sum reading with the same kappa and the falsifier (PD01 D3: 
    'any kappa strictly inside (1/2, 1) kills the structure') must be
    re-derived -- the slope-sum family fills (1/3, 2/3) continuously.

